.TH ESDM\-ES\-EXT\-RELAY 8 "2026-10-19" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
esdm\-es\-ext\-relay \- read an external entropy source on another host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B esdm\-es\-ext\-relay
.B \-\-connect
.I HOST[:PORT]
.RB [ \-\-cert
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-key
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-ca
.IR FILE ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B esdm\-es\-ext\-relay
serves the ESDM of its host an external entropy source of another host: a
provider program of
.BR libesdm_es_ext (3)
\- such as
.BR esdm\-es\-pkcs11 (8)
\- serving its source on the network, see
.B NETWORK
in
.BR libesdm_es_ext (3).
The
.BR esdm\-server (8)
does not reach the network itself \- it leaves the network namespace of its
host as it starts, and parses no certificate. The relay serves it a local
provider socket instead, which the ESDM reads through one of its provider
socket sources as any other provider
.RB ( "esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket" ),
and forwards each request over TCP with TLS and certificates:
.PP
.nf
	esdm\-server \-\-\-unix socket\-\-\-> esdm\-es\-ext\-relay
		\-\-\-TCP, TLS 1.3, certificates\-\-\-> provider program
.fi
.PP
The requests of the ESDM \- INFO, SELFTEST, GET_ENTROPY and STATUS, see
.BR libesdm_es_ext (3)
\- are forwarded one to one, and the answers of the provider passed back
unchanged: the ESDM shows the source under the name the remote provider
declares, credits it at the rate the remote provider declares \- unless one is
configured for the source \- and shows the status of the remote provider, whose
server tells
.B tcp
as its
.BR transport .
The relay answers STARTDTLS itself: the local socket has its own opportunistic
DTLS, as any provider socket, and the network its own TLS with certificates.
Every answer is checked as the ESDM checks it before it is passed back; one
that breaks the protocol ends the connection.
.PP
Every local connection has an upstream connection of its own, made when the
ESDM asks INFO \- which it asks first on every connection, and which the relay
requires first: the name and the rate the ESDM credits a connection with are
the ones the remote provider declared on it, so a provider restarted with
another declaration is taken at its new one with the next connection.
.PP
The remote provider being unreachable, or lost, is told to the ESDM rather than
hidden from it:
.IP \(bu 2
INFO, with the provider not reachable \- not resolvable, not answering, failing
the handshake \- is answered with the errno, and the local connection closed.
The ESDM shows the source as not connected and tries again after its retry
interval, at most every 5 seconds. A warning is logged once, until the provider
is reachable again.
.IP \(bu 2
An upstream connection lost while idle \- the provider was restarted, or closed
it as idle \- closes the local one: the ESDM connects again at once with its
next request, and so does the relay.
.IP \(bu 2
A request whose upstream connection fails or times out is answered with the
errno, and the local connection closed after it.
.PP
Nothing waits long: every step upstream \- the connection with its handshake,
or one request \- is bounded by
.BR \-\-timeout_ms ,
less than the 2 seconds the ESDM gives a request. A provider slower than that
is one the ESDM does without.
.SH TRUST
The ESDM accepts a provider by the user it runs as
.RB ( "esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket_uid" ),
and trusts it with the rate it declares. For the relay, the user stands for
what the relay accepts upstream: every holder of a certificate that chains to
the trust anchors of
.BR \-\-ca ,
is issued for the name of the provider and is not revoked by the CRLs of
.B \-\-crl
can declare a rate the ESDM credits. Give the relay trust anchors that issue
providers only \- a CA of their own \- or configure the rate of the source in
the ESDM
.RB ( "esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket_entropy_rate" ),
which takes precedence over the declared one.
.PP
The remote side can do nothing a local provider could not: the answers are
checked as the ESDM checks them, the claimed entropy of a block bounded by its
size, the status by the size of a provider status, and the requests are the
ESDM's alone. Another local client is served only when it runs as root or as a
user of
.B \-\-client_uid
\- see
.BR libesdm_es_ext (3)
on the slots of a provider.
.PP
The ESDM sees the relay as the user it ran as when it created its socket. Run
it as a user of its own \- as the shipped unit does \- rather than as root,
which the ESDM accepts without being told.
.SH TRANSPORT
TCP over IPv4 or IPv6, to port @ES_EXT_NET_PORT@ unless told otherwise; with
TLS 1.3 \- see
.B NETWORK
in
.BR libesdm_es_ext (3).
The relay is the TLS client: it presents its certificate, and verifies that
of the provider against the trust anchors of
.BR \-\-ca ,
that it is issued for the name of the provider \- the host of
.B \-\-connect
or
.BR \-\-server_name ,
a DNS name or an IP address \- and, if it has an extended key usage, for TLS
server authentication. With
.BR \-\-crl ,
the provider's chain is checked against the CRLs as the
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8)
checks its clients', see
.B REVOCATION
there: fail closed, as part of the verification of the TLS stack.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-connect " \fIHOST[:PORT]\fR"
The provider: a name or an address, an IPv6 address in brackets, as in
.BR [2001:db8::1]:@ES_EXT_NET_PORT@ .
The port defaults to @ES_EXT_NET_PORT@. The name is resolved at every
connection, and its addresses are tried in turn. Required.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-socket " \fIPATH\fR"
The local provider socket, which
.B esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket
names. A socket left behind by an earlier relay is replaced, one served by a
running process is not. The default is
.IR /run/esdm\-es\-ext\-relay/esdm\-es\-ext\-relay.socket ,
in a directory the unit creates (see
.BR SYSTEMD );
a relay started otherwise needs it to exist, or a socket of its own.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-mode " \fIMODE\fR"
Access mode of the socket, octal. The default is 0666: the ESDM connects as
.B nobody
once it dropped its privileges, and other users are not served anyway.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-client_uid " \fIUSER\fR"
Serve local clients running as
.I USER
\- a uid or a user name \- next to root. May be given up to 8 times; the
default is
.BR nobody ,
the user the ESDM runs as unless told otherwise
.RB ( "esdm\-server \-\-username" ).
.TP
.B \-\-no_dtls
Do not offer DTLS on the local socket. The TLS upstream is not affected.
.TP
.BI \-\-cert " FILE"
Certificate of the relay, PEM, optionally followed by the intermediate
certificates of its chain. It has to chain to the trust anchors of
.B \-\-ca
as well: the relay and the providers are issued in one trust domain.
.TP
.BI \-\-key " FILE"
Its private key, PEM, unencrypted, PKCS#8.
.TP
.BI \-\-ca " FILE"
Trust anchors the provider's certificate has to chain to, PEM. A chain ends in
a self\-signed one, see
.B \-\-ca
in
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8).
.TP
.BI \-\-server_name " NAME"
Name the provider's certificate has to be issued for, when it is not the host
of
.BR \-\-connect .
.TP
.BI \-\-crl " FILE"
CRLs, PEM; a file may hold several. May be given up to 16 times. Complete CRLs
only, no delta CRLs nor ones with an issuing distribution point.
.TP
.B \-\-crl_check_all
Require CRLs of the CA certificates of the provider's chain as well.
.TP
.B \-\-crl_allow_expired
Use a CRL past its nextUpdate with a warning, rather than refusing the
provider.
.TP
.BI \-\-reload_interval " SEC"
Check the TLS files for a change this often, and take them up when they
changed. 0 checks them on
.B SIGHUP
only. The default is 60. See
.BR ROTATION .
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout_ms " MS"
Bound of a step upstream: making a connection with its handshake, or one
request. The default is 1500, which leaves the relay time to answer the ESDM
with an errno within the 2 seconds it gives a request.
.TP
.BI \-\-max_connections " N"
Local connections at a time, each with an upstream connection of its own. The
default is 4, as many as a provider serves of one certificate by default
.RB ( \-\-max_per_client );
the ESDM holds one. With all of them taken, the client idle the longest is
disconnected to make room for a new one \- one running as root only for one
running as root, as the ESDM connects as root at start up.
.TP
.BI \-\-username " USER"
User to switch to after the socket is bound and the TLS files are loaded, when
started as root. The default is
.BR nobody .
The TLS files are read as that user when they are taken up later. The ESDM
sees the relay as root then, see
.BR TRUST .
.TP
.BI \-\-groupname " GROUP"
Supplementary group to switch to. The default is none.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
More verbose logging, may be repeated. With debug logging, the relay logs the
subject and the fingerprint of the provider of every upstream connection.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.PP
A file named without a slash is a credential of the service: it is taken from
.BR $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY ,
where systemd places what
.B LoadCredential=
names. Without
.BR \-\-cert ,
.B \-\-key
and
.BR \-\-ca ,
the credentials
.BR cert ,
.B key
and
.B ca
are used \- what the shipped unit loads.
.SH ROTATION
The relay takes up renewed TLS files without a restart: on
.BR SIGHUP ,
and when one of the files changes, which it checks every
.B \-\-reload_interval
seconds by the path, device, inode, size and modification time of each file.
Only files that load completely \- the key belongs to the certificate, the
certificate chains to the trust anchors, the CRLs parse \- replace the ones in
use; otherwise the error is logged and the relay goes on with what was loaded
before. New upstream connections are made with the new files; established
ones keep theirs. After every reload, the relay checks the providers of its
upstream connections against the new CRLs, and closes the connections of a
provider revoked \- one whose handshake began before the reload is checked
against the new CRLs once it completes; every 30 seconds, it closes those of a
provider whose certificate expired. Either closes the local connection as well,
and the ESDM connects again.
.PP
The credentials of
.B LoadCredential=
are copies made when the service starts; a rotation without a restart needs
the paths of the files, readable by the user of the service. The CRLs are
downloaded as described in
.B CRL UPDATES
of
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8).
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the TLS files at once.
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Close the connections, remove the socket and exit with status 0.
.SH SYSTEMD
.B esdm\-es\-ext\-relay.service
runs the relay as the system user
.B esdm\-es\-ext\-relay
(sysusers.d), serves
.IR /run/esdm\-es\-ext\-relay/esdm\-es\-ext\-relay.socket ,
loads the TLS files as credentials from
.I /etc/esdm/es\-ext\-net/client.crt ,
.I client.key
and
.IR ca.crt ,
and takes its options, the provider among them, from
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_RELAY_ARGS
of
.IR /etc/esdm/es\-ext\-relay.env .
It is ordered before
.BR esdm\-server.service ,
and after nothing of the network: the ESDM does without the source until the
provider is reachable. The ESDM is told about it with a drop-in:
.PP
.nf
# /etc/esdm/es\-ext\-relay.env
ESDM_ES_EXT_RELAY_ARGS="\-\-connect source.example"

# /etc/systemd/system/esdm\-server.service.d/es\-ext\-relay.conf
[Service]
Environment="ESDM_SERVER_OPTIONS=\-\-es_ext_socket /run/esdm\-es\-ext\-relay/esdm\-es\-ext\-relay.socket \-\-es_ext_socket_uid esdm\-es\-ext\-relay"

systemctl enable \-\-now esdm\-es\-ext\-relay.service
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
esdm\-es\-ext\-relay \-\-connect source.example \\
	\-\-cert /etc/esdm/es\-ext\-net/client.crt \\
	\-\-key /etc/esdm/es\-ext\-net/client.key \\
	\-\-ca /etc/esdm/es\-ext\-net/ca.crt \\
	\-\-socket /run/esdm\-es\-ext\-relay.socket
esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket /run/esdm\-es\-ext\-relay.socket \\
	\-\-es_ext_socket_uid esdm\-es\-ext\-relay
.fi
.PP
Two remote sources, for an ESDM with two provider socket sources: one relay
per provider, each on a socket of its own.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 after
.BR SIGTERM ,
1 when the options, the TLS files or the socket do not allow it to start.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libesdm_es_ext (3),
.BR esdm\-es\-pkcs11 (8),
.BR esdm\-server (8),
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Markus Theil <theil.markus@gmail.com>
